{
  "requirement": "Under {cite}`requirements.cli.skill_structure`, the `habeas-requirements` skill MUST be the home of drafting, reviewing, and agreeing requirements, the requirement review criteria, the procedure for choosing references, and the criteria the assessment applies to code and tests.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "**Obligations.** `requirements.cli.skill_structure.requirements` says the `habeas-requirements` skill MUST be the home of six topics:\n- drafting requirements\n- reviewing requirements\n- agreeing requirements\n- the requirement review criteria\n- the procedure for choosing references\n- the criteria the assessment applies to code and tests\n\nThe cited parent `requirements.cli.skill_structure` adds that each topic is stated in full in exactly one skill, and that a skill is its `SKILL.md` plus its `references/` files. So the tests must show two things for each topic. The skill states it, and no other bundled skill states it.\n\n**Test mapping.** The only test is `tests/authoring_cli.rs#fn[the_requirements_skill_is_home_of_drafting_review_references_and_criteria]`. It maps to the topics as follows.\n- **Drafting, reviewing, agreeing.** It asserts that the Workflow steps of `habeas-requirements`, parsed by `workflow_steps`, include text containing \"Draft. Write drafts\", \"Review. Sort the new and changed requirements\", and \"**Gate:**\".\n- **Choosing references.** It asserts that the `## Choosing references` section, extracted by `section`, contains \"A *root* is\".\n- **Criteria for code and tests.** It asserts that the `## What the review checks` section contains the phrases about \"obligations, boundaries, and failure cases no test runs and asserts\" and \"The supervising agent must still check that the code fulfills each applicable requirement\".\n- **Requirement review criteria.** It asserts that `references/review-prompt.md`, section `## Criteria`, contains \"**Compound:**\", \"**Checkable:**\", and \"**One reading:**\".\n- **Exclusive home.** It calls `assert_home(\"habeas-requirements\")`. That helper first asserts that the home skill, `SKILL.md` plus references, contains every marker from `home_markers`. For `habeas-requirements` the markers cover drafting (\"Write drafts from the request straight into the chapter\"), reviewing (\"For each group, start a read-only subagent\"), agreeing (\"**Gate:** no implementation or test code\"), choosing references (\"A *root* is an item that carries out part of a requirement\"), the \"## What the review checks\" criteria, the review prompt (\"# Requirement group review\"), and the \"**Compound:**\" review criterion. It then asserts that every other skill in `SKILLS` (`habeas`, `habeas-evidence`, `habeas-bootstrap-requirements`, `habeas-refactor`) contains none of those markers. That enforces the \"exactly one skill\" side for each topic.\n\n**Assertions.** The assertions are real (`assert!` and `assert_text` with labelled messages), and the helpers extract the right sections. `section` respects code fences and heading levels, and `workflow_steps` parses the numbered steps. All six topics, including the review criteria in the reference file, have a positive check in the home skill and a negative check against the other four skills. The separate test `bundled_skills_refer_to_a_topic_home_instead_of_restating_it` also checks that other skills point to `habeas-requirements` instead of restating its topics. Its snippet showed that it references `habeas-requirements`, but I did not read its assertions. My conclusion does not depend on it.\n\n**Residual limits.** The \"not restated elsewhere\" check is marker-based. A skill could in principle restate a topic in different words. That limit applies to any text-based check of \"stated in full\". The markers are distinctive sentences from each topic. I do not treat this as an adequacy gap.\n\nI found no unacknowledged gap.",
  "references": "f159a23beb16749e13e1d1de7c5be2c78dd6bcfcbb8168129bbd0ec2bacc21bf",
